ip20mon -- standalone bring-up monitor for the IP20 (Indigo R4000/R4400)
=======================================================================

This is the first step toward running this IRIS 3.6 tree on an Indigo
R4000.  The rest of the tree targets the 68020 IP2/PM2 boards; nothing
here is shared with it yet.  ip20mon proves the basics a kernel port
needs: the PROM can load us, we can drive the console, identify the CPU
and caches, find memory, and return to the PROM cleanly.

At startup it prints:
  - the firmware (ARCS version) and system name the PROM reports
  - CPU type and revision, primary and secondary cache geometry,
    the CP0 Config and Status registers
  - memory, both from the PROM's ARCS descriptors and from the MC
    memory controller's bank registers (the two should agree)
then gives an "ip20>" prompt:

  help     list commands
  info     firmware, CPU and memory summary
  cpu      CPU type, caches and status register
  mem      memory map from the PROM and the MC
  clock    measure the CPU clock against the PROM's clock (~2 s)
  env NAME show a PROM environment variable
  args     show the arguments the PROM passed
  trap KIND  raise an exception on purpose (no KIND lists them)
  regs     registers at the last exception
  vectors  exception vectors, the PROM's code there, cache line sizes
  reboot   reset the machine
  exit     return to the PROM


Exceptions
----------
Before the prompt appears, ip20mon copies a small stub to each R4000
exception vector (0x80000000 TLB refill, 0x80000080 XTLB refill,
0x80000180 everything else), saving the PROM's code there, and puts it
back on exit.  The stubs are written through the D-cache, so each
vector is written back from the D-cache and secondary cache and dropped
from the I-cache (the R4000 "cache" instruction) before it can be used;
ip20mon then reads the vectors back uncached to check memory has them.
Interrupts stay off while the vectors are ours; the PROM's console is
polled, so its I/O still works.

On an exception exc.S saves every register in a trap frame, leaves
exception level, and calls trap(), which prints the cause, EPC,
BadVAddr and registers.  Breakpoints and syscalls resume at the next
instruction.  Anything else abandons the command: trap() points EPC at
a recovery routine and erets to it (so the CPU really leaves exception
level), and that routine longjmps back to the ip20> prompt.  A bus
error also shows and clears the MC's latched error address and status.
An exception inside trap() is handled one level deeper; a third level
hangs, since nothing sane is left.

"trap" kinds: break, syscall, ri (reserved instruction), overflow,
div0 (gcc's teq divide-by-zero check), unaligned, null (TLB miss on
address 0) and bus (a load just past the last MC memory bank).

All eight were run on the Indigo R4000.  break and syscall resumed;
the rest reported the right cause (10, 12, 13, 4, 2 and 7) and returned
to the prompt, with "null" arriving through the TLB refill vector.
Afterwards "clock", "env" and "exit" still worked, and IRIX booted
normally on the restored PROM vectors.  What the run showed:
  - The PROM's own vector code loads a handler address from the ARCS
    parameter block (GEVector at 0x80001014, UTLBMissVector at
    0x80001018) and jumps to it; both point into the PROM.
  - The MC's bus-error interrupt is CPU interrupt line IP6, the only
    one the PROM leaves enabled.  It went pending on the bus error and
    dropped again once trap() cleared the MC's status registers.
  - Physical 0x18000000, just past 256 MB of RAM, is sent to the GIO
    bus: the MC latched it in the GIO error address with status 0x400,
    "GIO bus timed out", not as a CPU memory error.
  - Data bus errors don't set BadVAddr; the faulting address only
    comes from the MC (or from decoding the instruction at EPC).


Building
--------
This needs a MIPS cross compiler on a modern host; the 68k tools that
build the rest of the tree can't produce MIPS code.  On Debian/Ubuntu:

	apt install gcc-mips-linux-gnu binutils-mips-linux-gnu
	cd stand/ip20
	make ecoff		# makes ip20mon.elf and ip20mon.ecoff

Any big-endian-capable MIPS gcc works: make CROSS=mips-elf-.  The IP20
PROM only loads ECOFF, which mkecoff.py (python3) writes in the same
layout as NetBSD's and Linux's elf2ecoff; MKECOFF=elf2ecoff uses one of
those instead.  Don't use "objcopy -O ecoff-bigmips": it writes magic
0x140 for MIPS III code and keeps .rodata as its own section.

The program is linked at 0x88002000 (physical 0x08002000, the second
page of RAM), where NetBSD's IP20 boot loader also loads.


Booting
-------
From the PROM's Command Monitor (">>"), either over the network with
a BOOTP/TFTP server:

	>> boot -f bootp()server:/path/ip20mon.ecoff

or from a file in the disk's volume header, put there from IRIX with
"dvhtool -v creat ip20mon.ecoff ip20mon" (remove it again with
"dvhtool -v delete ip20mon"):

	>> boot -f dksc(0,1,8)ip20mon

"exit" returns to the PROM.  ip20mon talks to whatever the PROM uses as
its console, so to see it on serial port 1 set "nvram console d" from
IRIX (or "setenv console d" at ">>") first; "G" is the graphics head.

With only a serial line to an IRIX root shell, the file can be sent as
hex text and decoded with "perl -ne 'chomp; print pack(\"H*\", $_)'";
compare cksum on both ends.  Hex is safer than uuencode here, since a
System V tty may treat # and @ as erase and kill characters.


Hardware facts and where they come from
---------------------------------------
  HPC1.5 at 0x1fb80000; Z85C30s at +0xd00 (kbd/mouse) and +0xd10 (rear
  ports, port 1 = channel B); control/data at byte 3/7 of each channel
	NetBSD sys/arch/sgimips/hpc/hpcreg.h, hpc.c, dev/zs.c
  MC at 0x1fa00000, MEMCFG0/1 at +0xc4/+0xcc, SYSID at +0x1c
	NetBSD sys/arch/sgimips/dev/imcreg.h
  MEMCFG bank decode (base, size, valid; shifts depend on MC revision)
	Linux arch/mips/sgi-ip22/ip22-mc.c
  ARCS parameter block at physical 0x1000; firmware vector order;
  SGI memory-type numbering (2 = free contiguous, 3 = free)
	NetBSD sys/dev/arcbios/arcbios.h, arcbios.c
  IP20 board revision in bits 14:12 of 0x1fbd0000
	NetBSD sys/arch/sgimips/sgimips/machdep.c
  CP0 PRId, Config, Status layouts
	MIPS R4000 Microprocessor User's Manual

Tested on hardware
------------------
Run on an Indigo R4000 (100 MHz, 1 MB secondary cache, 256 MB, board
revision 5, MC revision 1, IRIX 6.5 on disk), loaded from the volume
header.  Everything matched IRIX's hinv:

	9536+16+16432 entry: 0x88002000
	firmware: ARCS 1.10, firmware vector 34 entries
	system: SGI-IP20
	cpu: R4000 revision 2.2 (PRId 00000422)
	  primary I-cache 8K, 16-byte lines
	  primary D-cache 8K, 16-byte lines
	  secondary cache, 128-byte lines (SC part)
	memory, as reported by the PROM:      total 131072K
	memory, as configured in the MC:      total 262144K
	  Count runs at 50.00 MHz, so the CPU runs at about 100 MHz

What that settled:
  - The IP20 PROM does provide an ARCS parameter block at 0x1000, with
    working Read/Write, GetEnvironmentVariable, GetMemoryDescriptor,
    GetChild and GetRelativeTime.  It also passes its environment
    (ConsoleOut=, OSLoader=, ...) as extra argv entries.
  - The MEMCFG decode from Linux's IP22 code is right for IP20 too.
  - The PROM's memory descriptors only cover the first 128 MB
    (0x08000000-0x0fffffff).  Memory above that, here a second bank at
    0x10000000, shows up only in the MC, so a kernel has to size
    memory from the MC rather than trusting the PROM.


Where this goes next
--------------------
Each of these replaces a piece of sys/ipII for the IP20:
  1. exception vectors and a trap handler (done, see Exceptions)
  2. TLB management with R4000 paired entries, in place of the IP2's
     hardware page map
  3. full cache maintenance with the R4000 cache instruction (ip20mon
     has the Hit operations for writing code; a kernel also needs the
     Index operations to flush whole caches)
  4. a clock interrupt from CP0 Count/Compare
  5. HPC1.5 DMA and the WD33C93 SCSI driver, so the kernel can read disk
